From: dArkOS RG52 Mini port
Subject: Castlevania: Lords of Shadow - Mirror of Fate: GammaOS's stereo switches, and no copies out of the right eye

Castlevania: Lords of Shadow - Mirror of Fate (000400000009E500) ran its
intro at 9 fps on an RG52 Mini. Measured on one stretch of it, frames 60 to
660 after a save state, every run from the same state:

- The game draws the two eyes object by object, each draw into the other
  eye's target. On the Mali GPU every switch ends a render pass, about 330 a
  frame: the GPU was busy 95% of the time and the driver's thread took most of
  a core. GammaOS's stereo_skip (off by default) learns the right eye's target
  from its transfer to the right framebuffer and leaves its draws out.
  11.1 fps.
- The game flushes the CPU cache before each of its command lists, about 15 a
  frame, and the core drained the GPU worker at every flush: the emulation
  thread waited for it half the time. GammaOS's async_gpu_flush_lag=24 lets 24
  jobs stay queued there instead (no wait at all measured the same, a lag of 8
  gave 11.7). 14.4 fps.
- The game copies each eye's image into one texture for its post-processing.
  The right eye's copy, out of a target nothing draws into any more, went to
  the CPU, which first downloaded the left eye's copy from the GPU (a wait)
  and had it uploaded again: three times a frame. With stereo_skip such a copy
  is now left out, as the right eye's transfer to its framebuffer already was.
  20.1 fps.

The two switches are environment variables here (patch 002). The core sets
them for this title in retro_load_game unless the environment sets them
already. Screens grabbed at the same frames match those without any of this.
The title ID is that of the owner's copy; the game's other releases have IDs
of their own, to be added when known.

